def f(i,k,c,r,s,o=None):qe(q,'io-multiplexing-q'+str(i),k,c,r,s,o)
e('Hardware was easily capable of it. The bottleneck was the operating system\'s interface for waiting on many connections at once.','Waiting interfaces, scheduling, memory and application design were among the scaling challenges.','C10K not single bottleneck for all workloads.',['https://www.kegel.com/c10k.html'])
e('shows why the later ones scale and the earlier ones don\'t.','compares their costs when many descriptors are idle.','Not universal old/new performance verdict.',ep)
e('        FD_SET(fds[i], &rfds);','        if (fds[i] < 0 ||\n            fds[i] >= FD_SETSIZE) abort();\n        FD_SET(fds[i], &rfds);','Prevent demonstrated fd_set overflow.',sel)
e('    select(maxfd + 1, &rfds,\n           NULL, NULL, NULL);','    int r = select(maxfd + 1, &rfds,\n                   NULL, NULL, NULL);\n    if (r < 0) {\n        if (errno == EINTR) continue;\n        perror("select"); break;\n    }','Do not consume sets on error.',sel)
e('int r = poll(p, n, -1); /* no timeout */\nfor (int i = 0; i < n && r > 0; i++) {\n    if (p[i].revents & POLLIN) {\n        handle(p[i].fd);\n        r--;\n    }\n}','int r;\ndo {\n    r = poll(p, n, -1);\n} while (r < 0 && errno == EINTR);\nif (r < 0) { perror("poll"); exit(1); }\nfor (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    if (p[i].revents != 0)\n        handle_events(p[i].fd, p[i].revents);\n}','Handle ERR/HUP/NVAL and syscall failures instead of spin/drop.',poll)
e('This fixes the 1024 limit (the array can be any length)','This removes the fixed fd_set bitmap limit (process and resource limits still apply)','poll not unlimited.',poll)
e('## Why O(N) per call is fatal','## Why scanning idle descriptors can be costly','Not always fatal.',poll)
e('Each `struct pollfd` is 8 bytes, so each call also copies 80 KB in and out of the kernel.','With an 8-byte struct pollfd, the input array is 80,000 bytes. In Linux v6.12, the output path writes each 2-byte revents field rather than copying the whole structure back: 20,000 result bytes. These counts exclude other access and scan costs.','Kernel source only puts revents back.',poll)
e('The server spends nearly all its time asking idle connections whether they are still idle.','The model performs much more scanning than useful event delivery; how much CPU time that consumes requires measurement.','No benchmarkproven dominatesCPU.',poll)
e('int ep = epoll_create1(0);','int ep = epoll_create1(0);\nif (ep < 0) { perror("epoll"); exit(1); }','Creation failure handling.',ct)
e('epoll_ctl(ep, EPOLL_CTL_ADD, lfd, &ev);','if (epoll_ctl(ep, EPOLL_CTL_ADD,\n              lfd, &ev) < 0) {\n    perror("epoll_ctl"); exit(1);\n}','Registration error handling.',ct)
e('    int n = epoll_wait(ep, evs, 64, -1);','    int n = epoll_wait(ep, evs, 64, -1);\n    if (n < 0) {\n        if (errno == EINTR) continue;\n        perror("epoll_wait"); break;\n    }','Do not silently spin on persistent errors.',ep)
e('        handle(evs[i].data.fd);','        handle_events(evs[i].data.fd,\n                      evs[i].events);','Forward HUP/ERR masks to handler.',ep)
e('in a red-black tree, so `epoll_ctl` add, modify and delete are O(log N).','indexed by a red-black tree in the cited Linux implementation. Tree lookup/update is O(log N); total epoll_ctl cost also includes allocation, locking and target-specific checks.','Complexity of index not fullsyscall bound.',src)
e('`epoll_wait` just takes items off the ready list. Its cost is **O(ready)**, independent of how many idle connections exist. Nothing is copied per call except the results.','`epoll_wait` processes ready-list candidates and rechecks readiness, returning up to maxevents results. It avoids an obligatory full interest-set scan. **O(ready)** is a useful simplified model, not an exact bound based only on returned events: stale candidates, contention and device callbacks add work.','Ready candidates can differ from returned events.',src)
e('| select | O(maxfd) | 1024 | None |\n| poll | O(N) | None | None |\n| epoll | O(ready) | None | In kernel |','| select | Scan model O(maxfd) | fd numbers < 1024 in glibc fd_set | No persistent interest set |\n| poll | Scan model O(N) | Process/resource limits | No persistent interest set |\n| epoll | Ready-candidate work | Process/watch/resource limits | Persistent kernel state |','Descriptor-number limit and real resource caps.',sel+poll+ep)
e('`epoll_wait` reports a descriptor every time it is called, for as long as the condition holds.','a ready descriptor remains eligible for reporting until the condition clears; maxevents, other ready descriptors and races affect which call returns it.','Not allreadyreturned every bounded call.',ep)
e('epoll reports a descriptor when its state *changes*, e.g. new data arrives. If you read only 1 KB of the 4 KB, epoll will not remind you. The remaining 3 KB sits there until more data arrives, possibly forever.','notifications arise from activity/readiness changes, and multiple events can coalesce. It is not a promise of exactly one notification per empty-to-nonempty transition. If you leave data unread, another reminder is not guaranteed; a connection can stall.','ET neither exactlyone nor onlybooleantransition.',ep)
e('Why bother with edge-triggered? It avoids reporting the same descriptor over and over, which saves wake-ups, especially for `EPOLLOUT` (a socket with send buffer space is writable almost all the time, so level-triggered `EPOLLOUT` fires constantly). nginx uses edge-triggered mode. Level-triggered is easier to get right, and libuv and Redis use it.','Edge-triggered handling can reduce repeated ready reports, especially for writable sockets. Level-triggered handling is often simpler; subscribe to EPOLLOUT only while needed. Library/server modes depend on version and backend, so inspect the actual implementation rather than assuming a universal setting.','Avoid unversioned framework defaults and guaranteed savings.',ep)
e('so two threads never handle the same connection at once.','as part of an ownership protocol: finish processing before rearming, and coordinate other code paths and duplicate registrations.','Oneshot notwholeprogrammutex.',ct)
e('wake only one of them rather than all.','wake one or more exclusive instances instead of necessarily waking all.','EPOLLEXCLUSIVE not exactlyone.',ct)
e('**epoll tracks open file descriptions, not fd numbers.**','**epoll registrations are keyed by both fd number and open file description.**','Both keyparts required.',ep)
e('**Edge-triggered behaviour** comes from the `EV_CLEAR` flag, one-shot from `EV_ONESHOT`.','**Edge-like behavior** can use EV_CLEAR, with filter-specific semantics. EV_ONESHOT deletes the event after delivery; EV_DISPATCH disables it for later re-enabling, closer to epoll\'s one-shot rearm model.','Deletion vs disable distinction.',kq)
e('the cost of the syscalls themselves (two per read: wait, then read)','syscall costs (wait results and multiple reads may be batched)','Not always2perread.',ep)
e('`select` scans every descriptor up to `maxfd`, copies bitmaps both ways and is capped at 1024 descriptors on Linux. `poll` removes the cap but is still O(N) per call.','glibc fd_set restricts select to descriptor numbers below 1024; scanning/copying costs depend on the range. poll removes that bitmap cap but still scans its input array.','Number vs count recap.',sel)
e('so `epoll_wait` costs O(ready), not O(N).','avoiding an obligatory full interest-set scan; actual wait work depends on candidate readiness, callbacks and contention.','Complexity recap.',src)
e('Level-triggered reports a descriptor while it is ready; edge-triggered only when its state changes, so you must drain to `EAGAIN` with non-blocking fds.','Level-triggered keeps ready descriptors eligible; edge-triggered does not promise reminders for unread data. Drain non-blocking I/O to EAGAIN or maintain your own pending-work queue.','ETcorrectrecap.',ep)
e('## Further reading','> [!note] Example scope and evidence gap\n> C snippets show registration and dispatch fragments, not complete servers. Supply headers, non-blocking descriptors, initialized arrays, cleanup and handlers that process data plus EOF/error/hangup conditions. Complexity and byte-count examples are models; no benchmark establishes universal connection capacity or CPU speedup.\n\n## Further reading','Visible missing empiricalevidence and code scope.',ep+poll)
f(1,'prompt','A Linux glibc server built on select corrupts memory after descriptor numbers grow beyond 1023. Which API misuse best explains this?','Specify libc/platform and actual descriptor threshold.',sel)
f(1,'explanation','The TCP stack is not capped at 1024 sockets by this API; actual capacity depends on resources and configuration.','Remove universal millions supported.',sel,2)
f(2,'prompt','Why must a select loop restore its intended fd_set before each call, either rebuilding it or copying a saved master set?','Rebuildnotsolemethod.',sel)
f(4,'prompt','Use a simplified one-scan-per-wake model: 50,000 registered connections, 50 ready candidates per wake, 2,000 wakes per second. Ignoring rescans, contention and registration work, compare poll scan entries with epoll ready-candidate processing.','Model notprecise kernelcost.',src+poll)
f(4,'workedExample','1. poll scan model: 50,000 × 2,000 = 100,000,000 entries/s.\n2. epoll candidate model: 50 × 2,000 = 100,000 entries/s.\n3. The modeled entry-count ratio is 1,000; it is not a measured CPU-speedup ratio.\n4. Assuming an 8-byte pollfd and Linux v6.12 copying behavior: 400,000 input bytes per call → 800 MB/s of input at this rate. The kernel writes 2-byte revents fields, totaling 100,000 output bytes per call → 200 MB/s of result fields, not 800 MB/s each way.','Fix actual outputcopy andmodel assumptions.',poll+src)
f(5,'prompt','A non-blocking EPOLLET socket already has all 4 KiB of a request buffered. After receiving the event, the handler reads 1024 bytes and waits only for another epoll event, keeping no local pending-work entry. The peer awaits a response. What correctness risk does this create?','Risknot deterministicTCPchunk notifications.',ep)
f(5,'text','The connection can stall with 3 KiB unread because another notification is not guaranteed','No guarantee alwayshangforever.',ep,1)
f(5,'explanation','Correct. Drain until EAGAIN, EOF or error, or keep unfinished work on your own queue so processing can resume without a new kernel notification.','Localreadyqueue alternative.',ep,1)
f(5,'explanation','A repeat notification is not guaranteed merely because data remains. This is the behavior an ET application must avoid relying on.','Not impossible newnotification.',ep,0)
f(5,'explanation','A further read would ordinarily find remaining data here. The bug is waiting for a notification that may not recur, rather than attempting that read.','Fix no nexttime absolute.',ep,3)
f(5,'workedExample','1. The event is delivered with 4096 bytes already buffered.\n2. One 1024-byte read leaves 3072 bytes.\n3. ET does not guarantee a reminder solely for these remaining bytes, so returning only to epoll_wait can stall the request.\n4. Further activity can produce notifications, but correctness cannot depend on that.\n5. Continue non-blocking reads to EAGAIN/EOF/error, or enforce fairness with an application pending-work list.','Binaryunits andrisk semantics.',ep)
f(6,'prompt','In the Linux implementation using a red-black-tree epoll interest index, what is the asymptotic cost of the index insertion/search component for N registrations, excluding allocation, locks and target-specific checks?','Whole syscallnotfixed complexity.',src)
f(6,'explanation','Correct for the balanced-tree component. Registration can also perform target polling, wait-queue setup, allocation and locking, whose costs are not captured by this tree bound.','Waitqueue callbacks not universallyoneconstant.',src,2)
f(6,'workedExample','1. The interest index distinguishes the pair of open file description and descriptor number.\n2. Balanced red-black-tree search/insertion takes O(log N) comparisons.\n3. log2(1,000,000) is about 20, indicating scale, not an exact count of comparisons or a bound on total syscall time.\n4. Registration includes additional work, and epoll_wait may inspect candidates that no longer produce returned events.','No exact20comparisons and returned count bound.',src)
f(7,'explanation','Successful keepalive probes are handled within TCP and do not deliver application payload. Keepalive is configurable, often not enabled on a socket at all; persistent writable notifications explain this scenario.','Remove unqualified defaulttiming.',ep,2)
f(8,'text','LT keeps a descriptor eligible for reporting while its readiness condition persists','maxevents preventsallready everycall.',ep,0)
f(8,'explanation','Correct. A particular bounded wait call may return only a subset of ready descriptors, and readiness can change before user code acts.','Batch/race scope.',ep,0)
f(8,'explanation','Correct. ET can avoid repeated reports solely because a condition remains true; notification counts and performance still depend on workload and event coalescing.','Not exactlyoneperchange.',ep,3)
f(8,'workedExample','1. LT keeps a nonempty socket eligible for reporting; each result batch is limited by maxevents.\n2. ET is activity-driven and can coalesce events. It does not promise exactly one event per empty-to-nonempty transition.\n3. Leaving unread data may not generate another reminder. Drain to EAGAIN, EOF or error, or keep your own unfinished-work queue.\n4. Non-blocking descriptors prevent the drain loop from sleeping when no more bytes are available.','ET andLT bounds.',ep)
f(9,'explanation','The registration key includes the original fd number and open file description. Its user-supplied data is stored separately and does not automatically change to the duplicate fd.','data notkey.',ep,1)
f(10,'explanation','Correct when one registration and a proper ownership protocol cover the connection. Finish processing before rearming; other I/O paths and duplicate registrations still require coordination.','Oneshotnotglobalmutex.',ct,1)
f(11,'workedExample','1. In the stated scenario a completed connection makes the listening socket readable, waking multiple epoll instances.\n2. One worker accepts it; others can find no pending connection and get EAGAIN.\n3. EPOLLEXCLUSIVE reduces this herd but documents one or more exclusive waiters, not exactly one. Nonexclusive watchers can still be notified.\n4. SO_REUSEPORT gives workers separate listening sockets/queues and distributes flows; it does not guarantee equal application work per worker.\n5. Non-blocking accept and correct drain/error handling remain necessary.','Exclusive oneormore andSYNnotwholehandshake.',ct)
f(12,'workedExample','Both APIs maintain registrations, but the mapping is conceptual:\n\n| epoll concept | kqueue counterpart |\n|---|---|\n| create instance | kqueue |\n| modify interest | kevent changelist |\n| retrieve events | kevent eventlist |\n| readable socket | EVFILT_READ |\n| edge-like handling | EV_CLEAR, filter-dependent |\n| disable after delivery | EV_DISPATCH |\n| remove after delivery | EV_ONESHOT |\n\nEPOLLONESHOT disables until MOD rearms; EV_ONESHOT deletes and must be added again. EVFILT_READ is readiness, not a completed socket read.','Kqueueoneshot semantics notidentical.',kq)
